<Base title="Books From Scratch: Adopting QuickBooks" description="What to expect when a business with no real accounting system adopts QuickBooks: the documents you'll provide, your role in setup, and the schedules your accountant builds.">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="hero hero-full hero-center">
      <span class="eyebrow boxed">What to Expect · QuickBooks Setup</span>
      <h1>Books From <span class="gold-text">Scratch.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">What it looks like when a business that has been running without real accounting moves onto QuickBooks, and why the owner's part in the setup matters so much.</p>
    </section>

    <article class="article">
      <aside class="glance">
        <h2>At a Glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Who it's for</dt><dd>Businesses tracking money through the bank account, a spreadsheet or the tax preparer once a year</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What you provide</dt><dd>Year-to-date bank, credit card, loan and financing statements, plus context on your transactions</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What we build</dt><dd>QuickBooks set up for your business, with loan schedules, fixed assets and depreciation, and accurate opening balances</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Where it leads</dt><dd>Reconciled books and a monthly close, managed by bkept from then on</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <h2>The Starting Point</h2>
      <p>Plenty of businesses run for years without a real accounting system. Revenue comes in, bills get paid, and the bank balance serves as the scoreboard. Records live in bank statements, a folder of receipts or a spreadsheet, and once a year the tax preparer pieces it all together.</p>
      <p>That works until it doesn't. The owner wants to know whether the business is actually profitable, a lender asks for a balance sheet, or the business takes on a vehicle loan or equipment financing and nobody is sure how to record the payments. That is usually when QuickBooks comes up.</p>

      <h2>Why Setup Is the Part That Matters</h2>
      <p>Opening a QuickBooks account takes minutes. Setting it up so the numbers are right takes real work. Software only records what it is told. If the opening balances are wrong, or a loan payment is recorded as a single expense, every report after that carries the error forward.</p>
      <p>A proper setup means the books start from accurate balances, and that every recurring item, such as a loan, a financed truck or a piece of equipment, is recorded the way it should be from the first month.</p>

      <h2>What We'll Ask You For</h2>
      <p>The setup is built from your own records. At the start of the engagement, we'll ask for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Year-to-date bank statements</strong> for every business checking and savings account.</li>
        <li><strong>Year-to-date credit card statements</strong> for every card used for the business.</li>
        <li><strong>Loan statements</strong> for any business loans or lines of credit, showing balances, rates and payment history.</li>
        <li><strong>Financing statements</strong> for vehicles, machinery or other equipment bought on terms.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Statements show what moved and when. They don't always show why. That is where you come in.</p>

      <h2>Your Role in the Setup</h2>
      <p>Owners are often surprised by how involved they are at this stage. No one knows the business's transactions better than the person who made them, so the setup is a working partnership between you and your accountant.</p>
      <p>Expect questions like these:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What was this payment for, and is it a business or a personal expense?</li>
        <li>Is this deposit a sale, a loan, a refund or money you put into the business yourself?</li>
        <li>When did you buy this truck or piece of equipment, what did it cost, and how was it paid for?</li>
        <li>Who is this vendor, and is the charge one-time or recurring?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Clear answers early make for faster setup and cleaner books. Most of these questions are asked once. After setup, rules and routines handle them going forward.</p>

      <h2>What Your Accountant Builds</h2>
      <p>With your statements and context in hand, the accountant builds the schedules that sit behind your books. These are what turn raw transactions into accurate financial statements.</p>
      <h3>Loan schedules</h3>
      <p>Each loan payment is split into two parts. The interest portion is an expense on the profit and loss. The principal portion reduces the loan balance on the balance sheet. A loan schedule tracks that split payment by payment, so your expenses aren't overstated and your loan balance matches what the lender says you owe.</p>
      <h3>Fixed assets and depreciation</h3>
      <p>Vehicles, machinery and equipment are recorded as assets, not as one large expense in the month you bought them. A depreciation schedule spreads their cost over their useful life, which gives a truer picture of profit and lines up with what your tax preparer needs.</p>
      <h3>Liability balances</h3>
      <p>Loans, equipment financing and credit card balances are set up with accurate opening balances and reconciled to the lender's and card issuer's statements, so the balance sheet shows what the business actually owes.</p>
      <h3>Chart of accounts and bank feeds</h3>
      <p>Income and expense accounts are organized around how your business works and how the tax return needs them. Bank and card accounts are connected through bank feeds, with rules for recurring transactions, and every account is reconciled to its statement.</p>

      <h2>After Setup</h2>
      <p>Once the books are set up and reconciled, the work moves to a monthly routine. Transactions are categorized as they come in, loan and depreciation entries are recorded on schedule, every account is reconciled, and you receive a monthly report with your profit and loss, balance sheet and a plain-language summary of what changed. Your involvement drops to answering the occasional question about something new.</p>

      <h2>What Changes for the Owner</h2>
      <p>For the first time, the business has numbers the owner can use: real profit, not just cash in the bank, an accurate picture of what is owed, and statements ready when a lender, partner or tax preparer asks. Decisions about pricing, hiring and new equipment start from facts instead of instinct.</p>

      <h2>Takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Gather statements first.</strong> Year-to-date bank, card, loan and financing statements are the foundation of the setup.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan to be involved.</strong> Your context on transactions is what makes the books accurate.</li>
        <li><strong>A loan payment is not one expense.</strong> Interest and principal belong in different places, and a schedule keeps them straight.</li>
        <li><strong>Equipment is an asset.</strong> Depreciation spreads the cost over the years you use it.</li>
        <li><strong>Setup is a project with an end.</strong> Once it's done, a monthly routine keeps the books right with little effort from you.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
